title: MfS Bezirksverwaltung Berlin, 1953
new-page: true

You are in the basement of basement of a Bezirksverwaltung, it is East Berlin. It is November, 1953. It has been five months since June 17th.

The building is a converted prewar office block. High ceilings, thick walls, a single bulb, you are thirty eight now. A Referatsleiter, a section chief, you have a desk, a file, a prisoner before you.

The man before you is a young man, a factory electrican, picked up after the sweep of the June uprising. He was seen at a strike meeting, he signed a resolution, he was reported on. 

He has been in the basement for eleven days, he has not slept, seen his wife, and asked the same question by three different men in three different rooms, he is starting to understand why there's no clock.

The file is thin, Werner K, member of the FDGB but not a parteilos. Werner has a wife, a mother, a two year olf daughter. The informer's report is three pages, in his own hand, names, dates, words used.

He is handcuffed to a table leg, a bruise on his left cheek. The man stopped asking for water, because asking is a kind of begging and he has decided not to beg, admirable, in a way.

"Werner," Says Erdman, you. "Your wife, Ursula, studying music is she? No one has seen you since Tuesday morning. Your daughter is no doubt worried sick."

The man says nothing, his jaw is tight, he is trying most hard to be dignified.

"Keep beign brave, Werner," Says you. "Stay here, if you will, and you will be put on trial. Your mother in the gallery, your wife as a witness and have to testify against you, your daughter without a father." Erdman stroked his chin, a small beard forming.

"Or you can talk with me here." The man was crying now, silently, the way men cry when they are trying most hard to make no sound.

You, though, feel nothing. You don't feel anything, you aren't paticuarily impressed or surprised. You merely found the right lever and now your job is about to be done.

"You aren't the first to sit in thi schair, nor the last. You can go home, though, and not come back." The man yields, he signs the documents on the table he's tied to.

You take the paper, Erdman, you read it, initials it, puts it away. You stand up and you leave, Werner shouting to be released. 

A man in the corridor, a guard, you say to him "Let Werner go tomorrow morning, give him a good night to think about it." Your word is law.

